  with the idea of the relationship she  had when she was 13 with a much older  man which at the time she thought was a  real relationship full of love and she  comes to realize slowly that it was  sexual abuse it's very beautifully  structured going back and forth through  her memories as her story that she's  been telling herself all these years  starts to crumble oh I definitely had  needed a lot of tissues in it another  film that I really enjoyed is called  Leave No Trace and this is directed by  Debra Granik who did Winter's Bone many  many years ago this is a return to  scripted feature films for her and once  again it's about a young girl this time  played by Thomas &amp; McKenzie a brilliant  young Kiwi actress and it follows her  and her father who's played by Ben  Foster as they navigate his PTSD after  being in the Army trying to live off the  grid in a park out of society and stay  together as a family it takes at a time  but it packs an emotional punch  really is well worth the work  and there's plenty more films here at  the Sundance Film Festival 122 features  in total 37 percent is directed by women  53 by news filmmakers it's incredible  and if you want to see more reviews make  sure you follow me on twitter at Alicia  Malone and I'll see you soon  [Music]

